    "commands": [
        "corepack pnpm nx run korfbal-django:loadtest --viewers=100 --matches=1 --seconds=60 --db-pool-size=8 --reconnect --output=<new-directory>",
        "corepack pnpm nx run korfbal-django:loadtest --viewers=100 --matches=1 --seconds=180 --db-pool-size=8 --reconnect --output=<new-directory>"
    ],
    "notes": [
        "Fresh sequential before/after runs at one and three minutes per phase (20 and 60 scheduled writes). Both pairs and failures are retained; short-sample tails are sensitive to outliers.",
        "Baseline runtime files were verified against merged main. The isolated baseline worktree has the corrected public_live cache alias and the previous opt-in profiler; the default workload/spectator modules are byte-identical. Its existing uv environment runs the exact command wrapped by the Nx target.",
        "This comparison excludes the separate scoring optimization in PR #1070.",
        "Final source also recognizes home_team_id/away_team_id update_fields aliases in match-save invalidation. That correctness-only signal branch is not exercised by the workload; measured source hashes are retained separately.",
        "Both runs use the isolated public_live cache alias with production timeout/retry options. No production services or real user data were used.",
        "The test suite ran separately from measured load. Python 3.14.7 differs from production Python 3.13; local API, worker and generator share host CPU. These are not production capacity limits.",
        "The workload includes summary, events, shots and statistics reads plus SSE setup; these remain database-backed. Request counts can differ as clients coalesce pending refreshes.",
        "A missed write slot means the generator skipped an arrival because the preceding action had not finished; it is not a failed or lost committed command.",
        "The 30-second bound applies to snapshot freshness on reads. Existing best-effort SSE delivery is unchanged; this change does not introduce a separate live worker fleet or claim 1000/10000-user capacity."
    ],
